{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sz9nxhsRAwzGc5qSUTQvsY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"Per-module coverage floors: stop the global floor hiding weak high-risk modules.\n3\t\n4\tThe global line-coverage floor (`docs/stack.ini` `[coverage] threshold`, enforced\n5\tby pytest-cov `--cov-fail-under`) is ONE aggregate number, so a heavily tested\n6\tgenerator can subsidize thin coverage in a security/process boundary while the\n7\theadline percentage still passes (repo-review 2026-07-22 M-4). This check adds\n8\tper-module MINIMUMS for the modules that must not be subsidized — initially at\n9\thonest current baselines, raised as focused behavioral tests land — and keeps the\n10\tglobal `--cov-fail-under` floor as the backstop rather than the only measure.\n11\t\n12\tIt splits along the same process/product line as `check_perf.py` (process.md §7):\n13\t**measuring** coverage is *product* work the project wires (pytest-cov emits a\n14\tJSON report); **comparing** each module against its declared floor is *process*\n15\twork the kit owns — this script, stdlib-only (arithmetic over JSON) and\n16\tstack-agnostic. Swap the JSON producer for your stack; the comparator is yours.\n17\t\n18\tInputs (a missing one degrades safely, never a false failure):\n19\t    --report  coverage.json         the coverage.py JSON report (pytest-cov\n20\t              `--cov-report=json`), keyed by source file with a per-file\n21\t              `summary.percent_covered`. ABSENT means coverage was not measured\n22\t              this run (e.g. the smoke tier, which the global floor also skips)\n23\t              => SKIP, the check_perf.py posture.\n24\t    --floors  docs/coverage-floors   one `<repo-relative module> <min percent>`\n25\t              per line — the docs/dupes-allow census idiom: `#` comments and\n26\t              blank lines are ignored, so each floor can carry a why-note.\n27\t\n28\tExit code is nonzero when any declared module is below its floor, OR is absent\n29\tfrom the report (a declared floor whose module vanished from measurement must\n30\tFAIL — never quietly pass), OR the floors file is malformed (a broken\n31\tdeclaration is loud, the same fail-closed stance check.py takes on a bad\n32\tprofile). Exit 0 when every floor holds, when no floors are declared, or when\n33\tthe report is absent (an unmeasured run).\n34\t\n35\tUsage:\n36\t    python scripts/check_coverage.py [--report coverage.json]\n37\t                                     [--floors docs/coverage-floors]\n38\t\n39\tWired via `docs/stack.ini` as a `[step:module-coverage]` gate step, so it runs\n40\tAFTER `tests+coverage` produces the JSON (check.py's plan orders extra steps\n41\tafter the test step). Opt-in like the dup-code gate: a repo without the step and\n42\tthe floors file pays nothing.\n43\t\n44\tContracts: IF-069, IF-070 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n45\t\"\"\"\n46\t\n47\timport argparse\n48\timport json\n49\timport sys\n50\tfrom pathlib import Path\n51\t\n52\t\n53\tdef _utf8_console():\n54\t    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n55\t    non-ASCII module path or a `<`/`>=` glyph can't raise UnicodeEncodeError on a\n56\t    legacy Windows cp1252 console. Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`;\n57\t    guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n58\t    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n59\t        try:\n60\t            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n61\t        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n62\t            pass\n63\t\n64\t\n65\tdef load_floors(path):\n66\t    \"\"\"Parse the floors census into `[(module, floor, lineno)]`. Each non-comment\n67\t    line is `<repo-relative module path> <min percent>`; blank lines and `#`\n68\t    comments (whole-line or trailing) are ignored, so a floor can carry a\n69\t    why-note. A malformed line — wrong field count, a non-numeric or\n70\t    out-of-range percent — raises ValueError naming the line: a broken\n71\t    declaration is a loud failure, never a silently dropped floor (the\n72\t    fail-closed stance check.py takes on a malformed profile).\"\"\"\n73\t    if not path.exists():\n74\t        return []\n75\t    out = []\n76\t    for i, raw in enumerate(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines(), 1):\n77\t        line = raw.split(\"#\", 1)[0].strip()\n78\t        if not line:\n79\t            continue\n80\t        parts = line.split()\n81\t        if len(parts) != 2:\n82\t            raise ValueError(\n83\t                \"coverage-floors line {}: expected '<module> <percent>', \"\n84\t                \"got {!r}\".format(i, raw.strip())\n85\t            )\n86\t        module, pct_s = parts\n87\t        try:\n88\t            pct = float(pct_s)\n89\t        except ValueError:\n90\t            raise ValueError(\n91\t                \"coverage-floors line {}: {!r} is not a number\".format(i, pct_s)\n92\t            )\n93\t        if not 0 <= pct <= 100:\n94\t            raise ValueError(\n95\t                \"coverage-floors line {}: floor {} out of range 0..100\".format(i, pct)\n96\t            )\n97\t        out.append((module.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"), pct, i))\n98\t    return out\n99\t\n100\t\n101\tdef load_report(path):\n102\t    \"\"\"The coverage.py JSON report as `{normalized-file-path: percent_covered}`,\n103\t    or None when the file is absent (the 'not measured this run' signal, like\n104\t    check_perf's absent metrics). Paths are normalized to forward slashes so a\n105\t    Windows-separated report key matches a repo-relative floor. Malformed JSON\n106\t    raises (a corrupt report is loud, not a silent pass).\"\"\"\n107\t    if not path.exists():\n108\t        return None\n109\t    data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n110\t    out = {}\n111\t    for key, info in data.get(\"files\", {}).items():\n112\t        pct = info.get(\"summary\", {}).get(\"percent_covered\")\n113\t        if isinstance(pct, bool) or not isinstance(pct, (int, float)):\n114\t            continue\n115\t        out[key.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\")] = float(pct)\n116\t    return out\n117\t\n118\t\n119\tdef module_percent(percents, module):\n120\t    \"\"\"The measured percent for `module` (a normalized repo-relative path), or\n121\t    None when no report key matches. A key matches when it equals the module or\n122\t    ends with `/<module>` — absolute-vs-relative and either path separator fold\n123\t    together, and the `/` boundary keeps `agent_session.py` from matching a\n124\t    hypothetical `my_agent_session.py`.\"\"\"\n125\t    if module in percents:\n126\t        return percents[module]\n127\t    suffix = \"/\" + module\n128\t    for key, pct in percents.items():\n129\t        if key.endswith(suffix):\n130\t            return pct\n131\t    return None\n132\t\n133\t\n134\tdef evaluate(floors, percents):\n135\t    \"\"\"`[(module, floor, measured_or_None, status)]` for each declared floor,\n136\t    status in OK / FAIL (below floor) / MISSING (no report match).\"\"\"\n137\t    results = []\n138\t    for module, floor, _lineno in floors:\n139\t        measured = module_percent(percents, module)\n140\t        if measured is None:\n141\t            status = \"MISSING\"\n142\t        elif measured < floor:\n143\t            status = \"FAIL\"\n144\t        else:\n145\t            status = \"OK\"\n146\t        results.append((module, floor, measured, status))\n147\t    return results\n148\t\n149\t\n150\tdef main():\n151\t    _utf8_console()\n152\t    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n153\t        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter\n154\t    )\n155\t    ap.add_argument(\n156\t        \"--report\",\n157\t        default=\"coverage.json\",\n158\t        help=\"coverage.py JSON report (default: coverage.json)\",\n159\t    )\n160\t    ap.add_argument(\n161\t        \"--floors\",\n162\t        default=\"docs/coverage-floors\",\n163\t        help=\"per-module floor census (default: docs/coverage-floors)\",\n164\t    )\n165\t    args = ap.parse_args()\n166\t\n167\t    try:\n168\t        floors = load_floors(Path(args.floors))\n169\t    except ValueError as exc:\n170\t        print(\"check_coverage: FAIL - {}\".format(exc))\n171\t        sys.exit(1)\n172\t\n173\t    if not floors:\n174\t        print(\n175\t            \"check_coverage: OK - no per-module coverage floors declared ({})\".format(\n176\t                args.floors\n177\t            )\n178\t        )\n179\t        return\n180\t\n181\t    percents = load_report(Path(args.report))\n182\t    if percents is None:\n183\t        print(\n184\t            \"check_coverage: SKIP - {} floor(s) declared but no coverage report \"\n185\t            \"at {} (coverage not measured this run - e.g. the smoke tier, where \"\n186\t            \"the global --cov-fail-under floor is likewise skipped)\".format(\n187\t                len(floors), args.report\n188\t            )\n189\t        )\n190\t        return\n191\t\n192\t    results = evaluate(floors, percents)\n193\t    for module, floor, measured, status in results:\n194\t        if status == \"OK\":\n195\t            print(\"  OK    {:6.2f} >= {:5.1f}  {}\".format(measured, floor, module))\n196\t        elif status == \"FAIL\":\n197\t            print(\"  FAIL  {:6.2f} <  {:5.1f}  {}\".format(measured, floor, module))\n198\t        else:  # MISSING\n199\t            print(\n200\t                \"  FAIL  (no measurement)  floor {:5.1f}  {} - absent from {} \"\n201\t                \"(module vanished or was excluded from coverage)\".format(\n202\t                    floor, module, args.report\n203\t                )\n204\t            )\n205\t    fails = [r for r in results if r[3] in (\"FAIL\", \"MISSING\")]\n206\t    n = len(results)\n207\t    if fails:\n208\t        print(\n209\t            \"check_coverage: FAIL - {}/{} module floor(s) breached \"\n210\t            \"(the global --cov-fail-under floor stays the backstop)\".format(\n211\t                len(fails), n\n212\t            )\n213\t        )\n214\t        sys.exit(1)\n215\t    print(\"check_coverage: OK - all {} module floor(s) hold\".format(n))\n216\t\n217\t\n218\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n219\t    main()\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66413f4b-e093-47b5-92ce-979e3a291f6c","uuid":"d7347c89-cb75-4d7d-b497-e07e4136d865","timestamp":"2026-07-23T21:44:07.380Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-279-b5fa\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\check_coverage.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Per-module coverage floors: stop the global floor hiding weak high-risk modules.\n\nThe global line-coverage floor (`docs/stack.ini` `[coverage] threshold`, enforced\nby pytest-cov `--cov-fail-under`) is ONE aggregate number, so a heavily tested\ngenerator can subsidize thin coverage in a security/process boundary while the\nheadline percentage still passes (repo-review 2026-07-22 M-4). This check adds\nper-module MINIMUMS for the modules that must not be subsidized — initially at\nhonest current baselines, raised as focused behavioral tests land — and keeps the\nglobal `--cov-fail-under` floor as the backstop rather than the only measure.\n\nIt splits along the same process/product line as `check_perf.py` (process.md §7):\n**measuring** coverage is *product* work the project wires (pytest-cov emits a\nJSON report); **comparing** each module against its declared floor is *process*\nwork the kit owns — this script, stdlib-only (arithmetic over JSON) and\nstack-agnostic. Swap the JSON producer for your stack; the comparator is yours.\n\nInputs (a missing one degrades safely, never a false failure):\n    --report  coverage.json         the coverage.py JSON report (pytest-cov\n              `--cov-report=json`), keyed by source file with a per-file\n              `summary.percent_covered`. ABSENT means coverage was not measured\n              this run (e.g. the smoke tier, which the global floor also skips)\n              => SKIP, the check_perf.py posture.\n    --floors  docs/coverage-floors   one `<repo-relative module> <min percent>`\n              per line — the docs/dupes-allow census idiom: `#` comments and\n              blank lines are ignored, so each floor can carry a why-note.\n\nExit code is nonzero when any declared module is below its floor, OR is absent\nfrom the report (a declared floor whose module vanished from measurement must\nFAIL — never quietly pass), OR the floors file is malformed (a broken\ndeclaration is loud, the same fail-closed stance check.py takes on a bad\nprofile). Exit 0 when every floor holds, when no floors are declared, or when\nthe report is absent (an unmeasured run).\n\nUsage:\n    python scripts/check_coverage.py [--report coverage.json]\n                                     [--floors docs/coverage-floors]\n\nWired via `docs/stack.ini` as a `[step:module-coverage]` gate step, so it runs\nAFTER `tests+coverage` produces the JSON (check.py's plan orders extra steps\nafter the test step). Opt-in like the dup-code gate: a repo without the step and\nthe floors file pays nothing.\n\nContracts: IF-069, IF-070 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef _utf8_console():\n    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n    non-ASCII module path or a `<`/`>=` glyph can't raise UnicodeEncodeError on a\n    legacy Windows cp1252 console. Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`;\n    guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n        try:\n            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n            pass\n\n\ndef load_floors(path):\n    \"\"\"Parse the floors census into `[(module, floor, lineno)]`. Each non-comment\n    line is `<repo-relative module path> <min percent>`; blank lines and `#`\n    comments (whole-line or trailing) are ignored, so a floor can carry a\n    why-note. A malformed line — wrong field count, a non-numeric or\n    out-of-range percent — raises ValueError naming the line: a broken\n    declaration is a loud failure, never a silently dropped floor (the\n    fail-closed stance check.py takes on a malformed profile).\"\"\"\n    if not path.exists():\n        return []\n    out = []\n    for i, raw in enumerate(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines(), 1):\n        line = raw.split(\"#\", 1)[0].strip()\n        if not line:\n            continue\n        parts = line.split()\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"coverage-floors line {}: expected '<module> <percent>', \"\n                \"got {!r}\".format(i, raw.strip())\n            )\n        module, pct_s = parts\n        try:\n            pct = float(pct_s)\n        except ValueError:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"coverage-floors line {}: {!r} is not a number\".format(i, pct_s)\n            )\n        if not 0 <= pct <= 100:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"coverage-floors line {}: floor {} out of range 0..100\".format(i, pct)\n            )\n        out.append((module.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"), pct, i))\n    return out\n\n\ndef load_report(path):\n    \"\"\"The coverage.py JSON report as `{normalized-file-path: percent_covered}`,\n    or None when the file is absent (the 'not measured this run' signal, like\n    check_perf's absent metrics). Paths are normalized to forward slashes so a\n    Windows-separated report key matches a repo-relative floor. Malformed JSON\n    raises (a corrupt report is loud, not a silent pass).\"\"\"\n    if not path.exists():\n        return None\n    data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    out = {}\n    for key, info in data.get(\"files\", {}).items():\n        pct = info.get(\"summary\", {}).get(\"percent_covered\")\n        if isinstance(pct, bool) or not isinstance(pct, (int, float)):\n            continue\n        out[key.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\")] = float(pct)\n    return out\n\n\ndef module_percent(percents, module):\n    \"\"\"The measured percent for `module` (a normalized repo-relative path), or\n    None when no report key matches. A key matches when it equals the module or\n    ends with `/<module>` — absolute-vs-relative and either path separator fold\n    together, and the `/` boundary keeps `agent_session.py` from matching a\n    hypothetical `my_agent_session.py`.\"\"\"\n    if module in percents:\n        return percents[module]\n    suffix = \"/\" + module\n    for key, pct in percents.items():\n        if key.endswith(suffix):\n            return pct\n    return None\n\n\ndef evaluate(floors, percents):\n    \"\"\"`[(module, floor, measured_or_None, status)]` for each declared floor,\n    status in OK / FAIL (below floor) / MISSING (no report match).\"\"\"\n    results = []\n    for module, floor, _lineno in floors:\n        measured = module_percent(percents, module)\n        if measured is None:\n            status = \"MISSING\"\n        elif measured < floor:\n            status = \"FAIL\"\n        else:\n            status = \"OK\"\n        results.append((module, floor, measured, status))\n    return results\n\n\ndef main():\n    _utf8_console()\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter\n    )\n    ap.add_argument(\n        \"--report\",\n        default=\"coverage.json\",\n        help=\"coverage.py JSON report (default: coverage.json)\",\n    )\n    ap.add_argument(\n        \"--floors\",\n        default=\"docs/coverage-floors\",\n        help=\"per-module floor census (default: docs/coverage-floors)\",\n    )\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    try:\n        floors = load_floors(Path(args.floors))\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        print(\"check_coverage: FAIL - {}\".format(exc))\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n    if not floors:\n        print(\n            \"check_coverage: OK - no per-module coverage floors declared ({})\".format(\n                args.floors\n            )\n        )\n        return\n\n    percents = load_report(Path(args.report))\n    if percents is None:\n        print(\n            \"check_coverage: SKIP - {} floor(s) declared but no coverage report \"\n            \"at {} (coverage not measured this run - e.g. the smoke tier, where \"\n            \"the global --cov-fail-under floor is likewise skipped)\".format(\n                len(floors), args.report\n            )\n        )\n        return\n\n    results = evaluate(floors, percents)\n    for module, floor, measured, status in results:\n        if status == \"OK\":\n            print(\"  OK    {:6.2f} >= {:5.1f}  {}\".format(measured, floor, module))\n        elif status == \"FAIL\":\n            print(\"  FAIL  {:6.2f} <  {:5.1f}  {}\".format(measured, floor, module))\n        else:  # MISSING\n            print(\n                \"  FAIL  (no measurement)  floor {:5.1f}  {} - absent from {} \"\n                \"(module vanished or was excluded from coverage)\".format(\n                    floor, module, args.report\n                )\n            )\n    fails = [r for r in results if r[3] in (\"FAIL\", \"MISSING\")]\n    n = len(results)\n    if fails:\n        print(\n            \"check_coverage: FAIL - {}/{} module floor(s) breached \"\n            \"(the global --cov-fail-under floor stays the backstop)\".format(\n                len(fails), n\n            )\n        )\n        sys.exit(1)\n    print(\"check_coverage: OK - all {} module floor(s) hold\".format(n))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","numLines":220,"startLine":1,"totalLines":220}}}
